Title: GOOD MOURNING
Credit: Episode 104
Author: Development draft
Draft date: 6 November 2026
Source: We Value Your Feedback
Notes: First draft. Target 9:45 story plus 0:15 credits. Timing requires table read and animatic.

# 104.1 | 00:00-01:00

INT. CANTEEN COUNTER - SUNDAY MORNING

A SPONSOR REP sets a new kettle beside the chipped one. The new kettle wears a detachable camera mount.

SPONSOR REP
Authentic moments. No direction. We just own the exclusive.

ISHA
The exclusive what?

SPONSOR REP
Experience. Your story, our platform.

ISHA
Whose story if someone changes their mind?

SPONSOR REP
We'd already have incurred costs.

Isha puts the new kettle back in its box.

SPONSOR REP
There's an equipment deposit.

ISHA
Then you should keep your equipment.

He leaves. Isha watches him take the deposit form with him.

Mara enters carrying paperwork.

MARA
You all right?

ISHA
Could have paid Nadi for three afternoons.

Mara looks toward the door, then at the canteen's private consultation screen.

She does not run after him.

# 104.2 | 01:00-02:30

INT. CANTEEN ACCOUNTS TABLE - MORNING

Tessa has three envelopes, three colors, and a ruler.

TESSA
Clinical fees. Still paid. Administration fees. Disputed, into escrow. This third envelope is the housing reserve.

SURI
Five thousand one hundred.

TESSA
If the residents vote to release it. Seventeen times thirty is five hundred and ten a day. Ten days. No more.

MARA
And the administration money—

TESSA
Stays in escrow. Not an extra purse because we dislike the company.

Isha turns over the window-repair estimate beside the reserve ledger.

ISHA
Whose windows?

TESSA
Yours. Mine. The north landing. This is what a bridge costs.

Suri reaches to sign the allocation. Stops herself.

SURI
Meeting first.

TESSA
Yes.

SURI
I know.

TESSA
That's why I'm saying it before you've done it.

Suri puts the pen in the middle of the table where everyone can reach it.

Mara adds her savings figure on a separate slip.

Tessa does not erase the collective total and replace it with Mara's offer.

# 104.3 | 02:30-04:15

INT. COOPERATIVE HALL - AFTERNOON

Nineteen voting residents. No stage. Mara is outside their semicircle and will not vote. Mara stands in the space where a chair has been moved for her.

MARA
The category that froze this building came from my work. I described people delaying renewal as unstable accounts. It made it easier to withhold payments until they complied.

MRS. DEV
Did you know it would do that?

MARA
I knew pressure improved completion. I didn't ask what the pressure was.

MRS. DEV
That isn't no.

MARA
No.

Mara lays down her disclosure and the savings slip.

MARA
These records go to the hearing, including my name. The money is an offer. It doesn't buy me a vote about what you do next.

SURI
The motion uses five thousand one hundred from our reserve. Ten days, capped. Administration fees go to escrow. Clinical payments continue.

MRS. DEV
And the windows?

TESSA
Delayed. Not canceled without another vote.

Silence. Real people deciding which problem they can afford first.

Hands rise. Tessa counts. Checks again.

TESSA
Eleven for. Eight against.

Suri writes a review date on the motion.

SURI
You need a negotiator for fourteen days.

MRS. DEV
Fourteen. Then we choose again.

Suri nods.

Mrs. Dev passes Mara on her way out.

MRS. DEV
I'm not thanking you.

MARA
I know.

She does not follow Dev to explain herself.

# 104.4 | 04:15-05:45

INT. PELL'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

An employment offer and a closure declaration sit side by side.

PELL
The regional post is still open. You understand how the system fails. That is an argument for putting you where you can improve it.

MARA
And the certificate?

PELL
Restored on closure. The message-source issue becomes a corrected labeling error.

She reads the declaration aloud.

MARA
No material harm resulted from the disputed processes.

PELL
We can change the wording.

MARA
Can we change whether it's true?

Pell folds his hands.

PELL
If this contract unravels, I have staff whose hours won't survive it. People who haven't made any of these decisions.

MARA
Which management hours are on the list?

He has no list ready.

PELL
This is not a simple punishment problem.

MARA
Then stop offering someone else as the punishment.

She pushes the declaration back unsigned.

PELL
I can't maintain your operational access during an adversarial proceeding.

MARA
My account exports are already lodged.

He looks up. She has anticipated the correct thing for once.

Her tablet changes: STAFF ACCESS SUSPENDED.

She removes the lanyard. Leaves it beside the unsigned offer.

# 104.5 | 05:45-07:15

INT. MUNICIPAL CLINIC ANNEX CORRIDOR - EVENING

Len stands at a roster. Several junior staff have written VOLUNTARY beside extra shifts. His own name is there too.

Mara arrives. He holds a folder against his chest.

LEN
Dad's treatment still bills through my supplement. If I lose this job—

MARA
I can't promise your job.

LEN
That's new.

He gives her the folder.

LEN
Nine hundred a month for messages. Six hundred for retention incentives. Nadi's paid transition plan is twelve hundred. Those local allocations could cover it.

MARA
Could?

LEN
If an order allows future reallocation. These are commitments, not banknotes. Don't announce we've found a treasure chest.

She writes FUTURE FUNDS — NOT CASH on the cover.

LEN
I'd have to explain the invoices myself.

MARA
Yes.

LEN
And staff get paid. Not a heartwarming paragraph about their generosity.

MARA
I'll put that in the request.

He watches her do it.

LEN
Then I can come.

They look at the unpaid-shift list. Neither tears it up. They do not yet have the money to replace it.

# 104.6 | 07:15-08:45

INT. CANTEEN KITCHEN - NIGHT

Isha finds Mara writing at a small table. The affected-households list now has reference numbers, dates, and gaps marked UNKNOWN. No patient details are exposed.

ISHA
Doing it in nice handwriting won't make it less bad.

MARA
I'm trying to make it checkable.

Isha reads the disclosure.

ISHA
You've written “unintended consequences.”

Mara crosses it out. Writes CONSEQUENCES I DID NOT ASK ABOUT.

Isha puts a bowl beside her.

MARA
I don't know what I want you to say.

ISHA
Good. You usually bring options.

Mara eats. Isha repairs the kettle's loose handle with a screwdriver.

MARA
The recorder. We don't use it tomorrow.

ISHA
I wasn't offering.

MARA
I know. I don't want to make her useful again.

Isha stops turning the screw.

ISHA
She was a person before she was our reason for doing things.

She tightens the handle. Leaves the switch alone; the fault needs a part they do not have.

A printer starts in the other room.

# 104.7 | 08:45-09:45

INT. CANTEEN - CONTINUOUS

Suri takes the page from the printer.

EXPEDITED GROUP CONTRACT REVIEW: MONDAY 09:48.
ANNUAL RENEWAL: MONDAY 10:00.
FILING DOES NOT STAY RENEWAL. AN ACKNOWLEDGED ORDER IS REQUIRED.

ISHA
Twelve minutes?

SURI
They've given us an appointment. Not a miracle.

Mara checks the electronic filing receipt beside the physical bundle. Len's witness confirmation appears beneath it.

Tessa packs the reserve motion and original contract in separate sleeves.

Isha coils the kettle's cable.

MARA
You're bringing that?

ISHA
Municipal clinic annex. Forty-seven people, one vending machine. Mum would've brought it.

She catches herself. No joke follows.

Suri places an extension lead in the bag.

Mara sets an alarm: MONDAY 09:30. Then another: 09:55 — CHECK ORDER ACKNOWLEDGMENT.

The four of them look at the packed bag. Nothing left to improve tonight.

CUT TO BLACK.

[[09:45-10:00 — End credits.]]
